Falsifies `credential_contract::proofs::no_request_word_makes_the_parse_read_outside_the_page`.

The defect: `id_len` reads the identity length from bits 20..36 of the request word
instead of bits 16..32, so client and server disagree about where that field is. The
client's `req` packs it at 16; the server slices the identity at whatever the shifted read
returns, so a login is attempted against a prefix, a suffix, or an empty identity, and the
wire format quietly has two readings.

Expected to fail: `assert!(identity.len() == ((w0 >> 16) & 0xffff) as usize)`, on any word
whose bits 16..20 are not zero.

**Measured blind before milestone 211.** The harness asserted
`identity.len() == id_len(w0)`, and `read` slices the page using `id_len(w0)`, so the
assertion could only ever say that `read` used the accessor. Confirmed by applying this
diff against the old phrasing and watching it stay green. The rewrite states the two
shifts, which is the wire format itself: DECISIONS' *move fast on what can be undone*
puts anything two programs agree on in the irreversible column, and a field offset is
exactly that.

The bounds assertions beside it (`identity.len() <= MAX_IDENTITY`,
`ID_OFF + identity.len() <= SECRET_OFF`) were independent already, and they are why this
defect is a correctness failure rather than a memory-safety one: the parse still stays
inside the page.

diff --git a/crates/credential_contract/src/lib.rs b/crates/credential_contract/src/lib.rs
index 39fae307..0a92d3de 100644
--- a/crates/credential_contract/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/credential_contract/src/lib.rs
@@ -238,7 +238,7 @@ pub const fn operation(w0: u64) -> u64 {
 /// The identity length a request word claims. Not yet checked against [`MAX_IDENTITY`]; that is
 /// [`read`]'s job, and it is the reason [`read`] exists rather than two accessors.
 pub const fn id_len(w0: u64) -> usize {
-    ((w0 >> 16) & 0xffff) as usize
+    ((w0 >> 20) & 0xffff) as usize
 }
 
 /// The secret length a request word claims. Same caveat as [`id_len`].
